1
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:51,950
PLANS FOR OPERATION WIENER ROAST WERE MADE HERE

2
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:54,950
AT THE ATOMIC ENERGY COMMISSION BUILDING IN IDAHO FALLS.

3
00:00:55,950 --> 00:00:59,950
AND THE EXPERIMENTS WERE CONDUCTED AT THE NATIONAL REACTOR TESTING STATION

4
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:03,950
53 miles away in the Valley of the Lost River Mountains.

5
00:01:05,950 --> 00:01:08,950
Although these pictures were taken in the middle of March,

6
00:01:08,950 --> 00:01:10,950
the peaks were still covered with snow.

7
00:01:11,950 --> 00:01:14,950
Careful preparations were made to approximate actual conditions,

8
00:01:14,950 --> 00:01:18,950
so the body of a real airframe was brought to the Twin Buttes Desert.

9
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:27,950
To record essential data, weather stations were set up

10
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:31,950
and direct gamma reading instruments strategically located.

11
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:39,950
From inside the airframe, the jet fuel oil can be seen at the bottom.

12
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:54,950
For better burning, holes were cut in the sides of the fuselage.

13
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:59,950
The nuclear fuel element had to be kept centered in the flame

14
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:03,950
so the jet fuel oil was arranged in a teardrop shape

15
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:06,950
and to check biological effects of the experiment

16
00:02:06,950 --> 00:02:10,950
arrangements were made with the local volunteer rabbit corps

17
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:26,950
They didn't mind a bit Intensity of radiation

18
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:30,950
was measured by direct gamma reading instruments placed three feet above

19
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:34,950
the ground. By using portable electrical instruments and mobile

20
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:38,950
power supplies, it was possible to get around and monitor the results

21
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:40,950
from almost any point in the test area.

22
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:45,950
Filters were used to sample the solid material in the air.

23
00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:50,950
Before and after both phases of the experiment,

24
00:02:50,950 --> 00:02:54,950
radiation measurements were made by means of walk-ins along eight radial lines.

25
00:02:56,950 --> 00:03:03,950
To study the upper air, blimps were set up to heights of five hundred, one thousand, and fifteen hundred feet.

26
00:03:04,950 --> 00:03:09,950
Attached to each were a series of vacuum jars, which were automatically opened,

27
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:12,950
samples taken, and then automatically recorked

28
00:03:12,950 --> 00:03:15,950
for analysis at a more convenient time.

29
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:27,950
The most difficult part of the operation was considered to be the accurate placing of the hot fuel element.

30
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:32,950
Proper test position was accomplished by using a crane with a 90-foot boom.

31
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:42,950
before the final decision was made to proceed with this experiment five trials were necessary

32
00:03:42,950 --> 00:03:49,950
equipment and procedures were studied personnel were briefed and detailed observations were made

33
00:03:49,950 --> 00:03:54,950
of the weather conditions the terrain and particularly the burning characteristics of

34
00:03:54,950 --> 00:04:03,950
jet fuel oil.

35
00:04:03,950 --> 00:04:07,950
It is a credit to the skill of the operator that he was able to put the fuel element into

36
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:14,950
a relatively small red funnel, despite an annoying 15-mile-an-hour wind.

37
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:20,950
This shows the position of the fuel element.

38
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:24,950
Before the experiment could be started, conditions had to be just right.

39
00:04:24,950 --> 00:04:31,950
everything had to be checked and rechecked.

40
00:04:31,950 --> 00:04:35,950
The instrumentation was made within a 45 degree sector.

41
00:04:35,950 --> 00:04:46,950
If the experiment was repeated, this would be increased to 90 or perhaps even 180 degrees.

42
00:04:46,950 --> 00:04:51,950
Unfavorable winds caused delay, and smudge pots were set out to determine whether the

43
00:04:51,950 --> 00:04:57,950
the wind on the ground was the same as indicated by the blimps.

44
00:04:57,950 --> 00:05:03,950
After postponing the experiment for four successive days, word was given to begin, and the first

45
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:16,950
blimp was sent aloft to its designated height.

46
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:21,950
thermite starters were needed to set the oil on fire, because jet fuel has a tendency to

47
00:05:21,950 --> 00:05:28,950
develop resistance to combustion when it remains overnight in cold weather. Previous attempts

48
00:05:28,950 --> 00:05:33,950
with black powder proved to be ineffective, so a battery of five thermite starters were

49
00:05:33,950 --> 00:05:41,950
installed, and each one of them ignited successfully.

50
00:05:41,950 --> 00:05:47,950
When ignited, the blaze is clearly visible, but automobile tires were needed to produce

51
00:05:47,950 --> 00:05:49,950
a large volume of dense black smoke.

52
00:06:00,950 --> 00:06:06,950
As the jet fuel oil burns vigorously, it envelops the nuclear fuel element.

53
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:33,950
The purpose of Phase A was to simulate the crash of a nuclear-powered aircraft.

54
00:06:33,950 --> 00:06:38,950
The airframe used in the experiment is the fuselage of an actual plane.

55
00:06:38,950 --> 00:06:40,950
The skin is made of magnesium.

56
00:06:40,950 --> 00:06:43,950
And the supporting members are aluminum.

57
00:06:44,950 --> 00:06:47,950
The white smoke is the metallic oxide created by the intense heat.

58
00:06:57,950 --> 00:07:01,950
As the inferno continued, most of the magnesium and aluminum melted.

59
00:07:02,950 --> 00:07:04,950
Most of it did not burn away.

60
00:07:10,950 --> 00:07:21,950
This quadropod rig was custom designed to help keep the fuel element upright throughout the experiment.

61
00:07:22,950 --> 00:07:29,950
But without consulting any of the scientists, a workman substituted a brass nut for one of the steel ones he had damaged.

62
00:07:30,950 --> 00:07:36,950
As you will see later on, the brass nut melted and one of the legs of the quadropod collapsed.

63
00:07:36,950 --> 00:07:39,950
This caused difficulty in precise evaluation of data.

64
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:53,950
the flame continued

65
00:07:53,950 --> 00:07:56,950
for almost two hours and as the fire

66
00:07:56,950 --> 00:08:01,950
raged furiously the temperature soared to 2250 degrees Fahrenheit

67
00:08:01,950 --> 00:08:04,950
higher than in any of the previous trials

68
00:08:04,950 --> 00:08:08,950
by at least another hundred degrees it should be noted

69
00:08:08,950 --> 00:08:12,950
that none of the preliminary work involved the use of actual materials.

70
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:17,950
Prior to Operation Wiener Roast, the mock-ups did not include an airframe,

71
00:08:18,950 --> 00:08:21,950
nor was the nuclear fuel element included in the stainless steel container.

72
00:08:23,950 --> 00:08:26,950
Not until final agreement was reached by the planning committee

73
00:08:26,950 --> 00:08:30,950
was any scientific attempt made to determine what would happen

74
00:08:30,950 --> 00:08:34,950
if a radioactive fuel element was subjected to conditions

75
00:08:34,950 --> 00:08:37,950
simulated by the crash of a nuclear-powered plane.

76
00:08:37,950 --> 00:08:44,950
with direct gamma reading instruments, fallout plates, and the rabbits acting as biological

77
00:08:44,950 --> 00:08:52,950
indicators, air samples were taken as far as one mile downwind. It had been anticipated that if

78
00:08:52,950 --> 00:08:58,950
there was a comparatively small amount of radioactivity, about five or ten percent, the loss

79
00:08:58,950 --> 00:09:05,950
could be easily verified. All that was required was to check the results of the walk-ins before

80
00:09:05,950 --> 00:09:10,950
and after the experiment. Even though the wind kept shifting back and forth through

81
00:09:10,950 --> 00:09:16,950
approximately 40 degrees, there was no difficulty in obtaining all the essential data.

82
00:09:23,950 --> 00:09:28,950
The first time this experiment was mocked up, a circular pond of jet fuel oil was used,

83
00:09:28,950 --> 00:09:35,950
but with a 12 mile an hour wind, the fire stayed beyond the end of the container. However,

84
00:09:35,950 --> 00:09:39,950
with a teardrop shape, a maximum flame was maintained around the fuel element.

85
00:09:44,950 --> 00:09:49,950
At the bottom of the fuselage, the white metallic oxide flame may be seen. This,

86
00:09:49,950 --> 00:09:55,950
together with the automobile tires, made quite a sight, visible all over the upper Snake River Valley.

87
00:09:55,950 --> 00:10:06,950
The ground wind at the time had a swing of close to 20 degrees.

88
00:10:07,950 --> 00:10:10,950
The test crew was fully prepared for this set of circumstances

89
00:10:10,950 --> 00:10:13,950
and took the precaution of tying the blimps to trucks,

90
00:10:13,950 --> 00:10:18,950
which just had to move back and forth to keep the apparatus in the main plume of the smoke.

91
00:10:25,950 --> 00:10:44,950
The airframe was expected to melt down completely and collapse,

92
00:10:45,950 --> 00:10:50,950
but the quadropod was carefully designed to hold the fuel element in a constantly vertical position

93
00:10:50,950 --> 00:10:53,950
to allow for the walk-ins along the radials

94
00:10:53,950 --> 00:10:56,950
for checking the loss of radioactive materials.

95
00:10:57,950 --> 00:11:03,950
However, when one of the legs gave way, the fuel element fell and the radial picture was distorted.

96
00:11:03,950 --> 00:11:07,950
For a time, it appeared that the loss was as high as 50 percent

97
00:11:07,950 --> 00:11:10,950
because the radiation field went down immediately.

98
00:11:11,950 --> 00:11:14,950
The truth was discovered before the experiment was concluded.

99
00:11:15,950 --> 00:11:18,950
That ring is the top of the stainless steel rope

100
00:11:18,950 --> 00:11:19,950
holding the fuel element.

101
00:11:19,950 --> 00:11:24,950
In a certain sense, it's almost unfortunate

102
00:11:24,950 --> 00:11:27,950
that there was no release of radioactivity

103
00:11:27,950 --> 00:11:31,950
No opportunity was presented to measure the spread of radioactivity

104
00:11:31,950 --> 00:11:35,950
Originally, it was assumed that if the nuclear fuel element

105
00:11:35,950 --> 00:11:41,950
did catch on fire and burn radioactive materials would follow in the trail of the smoke

106
00:11:41,950 --> 00:11:44,950
This conclusion is still not certain

107
00:12:19,950 --> 00:12:32,950
At the end of the experiment,

108
00:12:32,950 --> 00:12:34,950
only one problem remained unanswered.

109
00:12:35,950 --> 00:12:38,950
How could the fuel element be safely removed

110
00:12:38,950 --> 00:12:40,950
from the molten mass of magnesium and aluminum?

111
00:12:49,950 --> 00:13:03,950
After examining the nature of the melted metal,

112
00:13:03,950 --> 00:13:07,950
some of the crew went back to the shop and made a couple of special tools.

113
00:13:08,950 --> 00:13:11,950
This one is a long stick with a hook on the end.

114
00:13:11,950 --> 00:13:17,950
And with the use of manpower alone, the fuel element was pulled loose from the solidified metal.

115
00:13:20,950 --> 00:13:27,950
This view is somewhat distorted because the telephoto lens makes the men appear closer than they actually were.

116
00:13:33,950 --> 00:13:36,950
The other tool made in the instrument shop was a grappling hook,

117
00:13:37,950 --> 00:13:40,950
now being held at just the right angle by those ropes,

118
00:13:40,950 --> 00:13:42,950
so that the crane can pick up the fuel element.

119
00:13:49,950 --> 00:14:08,950
This tricky operation took only an hour and a half

120
00:14:08,950 --> 00:14:12,950
because the operator had such acute depth perception.

121
00:14:13,950 --> 00:14:15,950
That time includes the making of the tools.

122
00:14:15,950 --> 00:14:23,950
in addition to some dirt on the fuel element formed by a coat of melted magnesium and aluminum

123
00:14:23,950 --> 00:14:30,950
a small triangular hole was discovered in the cladding this indicated a puncture when the

124
00:14:30,950 --> 00:14:35,950
fallout plates were developed the amount of radioactivity indicated was insignificant

125
00:14:35,950 --> 00:14:41,950
the air samples registered nothing with the exception of the two places where the fuel

126
00:14:41,950 --> 00:14:45,950
element fell survey instruments could not detect anything

127
00:14:51,950 --> 00:14:54,950
incidentally the shape of the fuel element is still classified

128
00:14:54,950 --> 00:14:56,950
so there are no close-ups of this procedure

129
00:14:59,950 --> 00:15:04,950
another thing not shown in this film was the initial attempt to drop the fuel element into

130
00:15:04,950 --> 00:15:08,950
the garbage can it fell off the hook the first time and had to be done over

131
00:15:09,950 --> 00:15:12,950
This is the second time around. There was no difficulty.

132
00:15:17,950 --> 00:15:21,950
The garbage can was then put into a shielded container

133
00:15:21,950 --> 00:15:25,950
and taken to the hot room where there were facilities for comprehensive examination.

134
00:15:29,950 --> 00:15:34,950
Results of phase A demonstrated that there is no release of radioactivity

135
00:15:34,950 --> 00:15:39,950
when a nuclear fuel element has been subjected to the intense heat of burning jet fuel oil

136
00:15:39,950 --> 00:15:44,950
combined with other reactor materials under these test conditions.

137
00:15:58,950 --> 00:16:03,950
The second phase of the experiment was organized to determine the effects of extreme heat

138
00:16:03,950 --> 00:16:05,950
on a nuclear fuel element.

139
00:16:06,950 --> 00:16:10,950
This time it was planned to raise the temperature to 5,600 degrees Fahrenheit.

140
00:16:11,950 --> 00:16:16,950
And the curies were increased to 10,000, twice the amount used in Phase A.

141
00:16:17,950 --> 00:16:20,950
Although no radioactive materials were used during the trials,

142
00:16:21,950 --> 00:16:24,950
everyone had a chance to preview the nature of the events

143
00:16:24,950 --> 00:16:26,950
to be observed during the actual experiment.

144
00:16:33,950 --> 00:16:40,950
The demanding nature of Phase B required about 10 mock-ups like this

145
00:16:40,950 --> 00:16:44,950
to guarantee an enclosure that would last a minute and a half.

146
00:17:03,950 --> 00:17:25,950
If there had been any contamination of the area,

147
00:17:26,950 --> 00:17:30,950
previous arrangements had been made to use another site for Phase B.

148
00:17:31,950 --> 00:17:35,950
Actually, the only changes needed were fresh filter papers and a new set of rabbits.

149
00:17:36,950 --> 00:17:38,950
The instrumentation remained the same.

150
00:17:44,950 --> 00:17:47,950
The test pipes were lined with close to three inches of magnesium,

151
00:17:49,950 --> 00:17:52,950
filled with iron filings, steel wool, and thermite.

152
00:17:52,950 --> 00:18:01,950
To intensify the heat, oxygen was added by means of a cylinder connected to the pipe

153
00:18:01,950 --> 00:18:06,950
and the valves were turned mechanically from a distance of 200 yards.

154
00:18:52,950 --> 00:19:00,950
As the experiment proceeded, not all the well-rehearsed movements ran smoothly.

155
00:19:01,950 --> 00:19:04,950
For one thing, the wind freshened up to about 20 miles an hour,

156
00:19:04,950 --> 00:19:09,950
and the crane operator had trouble hitting the funnel, even though it was painted red.

157
00:19:10,950 --> 00:19:13,950
To further complicate the situation, the fuel element stuck twice

158
00:19:13,950 --> 00:19:17,950
before it was successfully lowered down into the interior of the pipe.

159
00:19:17,950 --> 00:19:23,950
Despite these minor delays, the second phase of the experiment began within an

160
00:19:23,950 --> 00:19:27,950
hour and a half after the completion of Phase A.

161
00:19:36,950 --> 00:19:42,950
After the preliminary radial walk-ins, the thermite charge was set off, and the

162
00:19:42,950 --> 00:19:45,950
first smoke went sailing in a direction which the rest of the smoke did not

163
00:19:45,950 --> 00:19:53,950
follow. This went all the way to the Westinghouse plant, five miles in the distance. This is a

164
00:19:53,950 --> 00:19:59,950
closer view of the thermite starter. The closer shots appear darker because of differences in

165
00:19:59,950 --> 00:20:05,950
photographic exposure among the three cameras. With the exception of the smoke which drifted

166
00:20:05,950 --> 00:20:10,950
to the Westinghouse plant, everything went according to plan and resembled the pattern

167
00:20:10,950 --> 00:20:11,950
of the trial mock-ups.

168
00:20:13,950 --> 00:20:18,950
Watching from the field as dust descended, it looked like a spectacular display of fireworks,

169
00:20:19,950 --> 00:20:20,950
complete with Roman candle.

170
00:20:23,950 --> 00:20:24,950
But there were drawbacks, too.

171
00:20:25,950 --> 00:20:28,950
None of the blimps was in a position to get an air sample.

172
00:20:29,950 --> 00:20:32,950
They were all too high and out of the path of the downwind.

173
00:20:33,950 --> 00:20:37,950
This was somewhat disappointing because great pains had been taken

174
00:20:37,950 --> 00:20:41,950
to make sure the survey instruments would be able to record the results.

175
00:20:42,950 --> 00:20:47,950
Since radioactivity can easily be detected if there is 1,000 curies per distance mile,

176
00:20:48,950 --> 00:20:50,950
10,000 curies were put into that torch.

177
00:20:52,950 --> 00:20:57,950
In this phase of the experiment, the integrity of the fuel element was completely violated,

178
00:20:58,950 --> 00:21:01,950
and the release was estimated to be up to 10%.

179
00:21:01,950 --> 00:21:05,950
The great bulk of the activity remained within a radius of 50 feet,

180
00:21:06,950 --> 00:21:12,950
but with survey instruments, it was discovered on the ground and on sagebrush for about 2,000 feet.

181
00:21:13,950 --> 00:21:17,950
Activity was well contained in a plume of about 25 degrees

182
00:21:17,950 --> 00:21:20,950
and couldn't be found beyond 2,000 feet,

183
00:21:21,950 --> 00:21:25,950
except for the air filter samples in the neighborhood of the Westinghouse site.

184
00:21:26,950 --> 00:21:30,950
Nothing definite could be determined from the radial walk-ins after the experiment

185
00:21:30,950 --> 00:21:32,950
because of the shift in the winds.

186
00:21:33,950 --> 00:21:37,950
All that could be determined was proof of fission products all over the ground.

187
00:21:38,950 --> 00:21:42,950
Final lab reports later on proved the radioactive material

188
00:21:42,950 --> 00:21:46,950
was largely ruthenium, cesium, and the halogens, including iodine.

189
00:21:47,950 --> 00:21:52,950
It was all over in 90 seconds, but it was an unforgettable experience.

190
00:21:53,950 --> 00:21:57,950
Next day, the whole site was cleaned up in less than three hours.

191
00:21:57,950 --> 00:22:01,950
The area was bulldozed, the earth put into a dumpster truck trailer,

192
00:22:01,950 --> 00:22:03,950
and then hauled to the burial ground.

193
00:22:08,950 --> 00:22:11,950
Results of Phase B of Operation Wiener Roast

194
00:22:11,950 --> 00:22:16,950
indicated that with a temperature of approximately 5,000 degrees Fahrenheit

195
00:22:16,950 --> 00:22:23,950
and under the test conditions, the upper limit of radioactivity released was 10%,

196
00:22:23,950 --> 00:22:28,950
with most of this staying within a half mile of the release point.
